WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.375
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:18.305
Unit thirty-six: Persuasion in Politics. In this unit, you will recognise and analyse persuasive techniques in political language.

4
00:00:19.317 --> 00:00:20.608
political rhetoric.

5
00:00:21.308 --> 00:00:26.245
political rhetoric means language used by politicians to persuade and inspire.

6
00:00:26.545 --> 00:00:29.810
Political rhetoric can unite a nation or divide it.

7
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.778
Your turn. Say: political rhetoric.

8
00:00:36.478 --> 00:00:37.769
political rhetoric.

9
00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:40.179
campaign messaging.

10
00:00:40.879 --> 00:00:45.746
campaign messaging means the planned words and themes a campaign repeats to voters.

11
00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:49.149
Their campaign messaging focused on jobs and safety.

12
00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:53.140
Your turn. Say: campaign messaging.

13
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.189
campaign messaging.

14
00:00:58.183 --> 00:00:59.138
spin doctor.

15
00:00:59.838 --> 00:01:05.994
spin doctor means an adviser who presents news in the most favourable way for a politician.

16
00:01:06.294 --> 00:01:10.267
The spin doctor called the defeat 'a learning opportunity'.

17
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:14.154
Your turn. Say: spin doctor.

18
00:01:16.854 --> 00:01:17.808
spin doctor.

19
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:20.027
talking point.

20
00:01:20.727 --> 00:01:24.654
talking point means a short prepared argument that speakers repeat.

21
00:01:24.954 --> 00:01:28.764
Every minister repeated the same talking point on television.

22
00:01:29.464 --> 00:01:32.593
Your turn. Say: talking point.

23
00:01:35.293 --> 00:01:36.363
talking point.

24
00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:38.409
soundbite.

25
00:01:39.109 --> 00:01:43.187
soundbite means a short, memorable phrase designed for the media.

26
00:01:43.487 --> 00:01:45.904
'Yes we can' was a powerful soundbite.

27
00:01:46.604 --> 00:01:49.524
Your turn. Say: soundbite.

28
00:01:52.224 --> 00:01:53.364
soundbite.

29
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.036
dog-whistle politics.

30
00:01:56.736 --> 00:02:02.428
dog-whistle politics means coded messages that only certain groups are meant to understand.

31
00:02:02.728 --> 00:02:06.887
Dog-whistle politics lets candidates deny what they really mean.

32
00:02:07.587 --> 00:02:10.994
Your turn. Say: dog-whistle politics.

33
00:02:13.694 --> 00:02:15.507
dog-whistle politics.

34
00:02:16.517 --> 00:02:18.052
populist rhetoric.

35
00:02:18.752 --> 00:02:23.828
populist rhetoric means language that sets 'the ordinary people' against a corrupt elite.

36
00:02:24.128 --> 00:02:28.124
Populist rhetoric grows when trust in institutions falls.

37
00:02:28.824 --> 00:02:32.452
Your turn. Say: populist rhetoric.

38
00:02:35.152 --> 00:02:36.664
populist rhetoric.

39
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.092
electoral strategy.

40
00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:44.009
electoral strategy means a plan for winning votes in an election.

41
00:02:44.309 --> 00:02:47.702
Their electoral strategy targeted young urban voters.

42
00:02:48.402 --> 00:02:51.739
Your turn. Say: electoral strategy.

43
00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:55.986
electoral strategy.

44
00:02:56.983 --> 00:02:59.180
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:03.094
Ms. Sarah, why do politicians repeat the same phrases?

46
00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:08.159
Because a talking point repeated often enough begins to feel true.

47
00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:12.193
So campaign messaging is mostly repetition?

48
00:03:13.483 --> 00:03:19.328
Repetition and emotion. A great soundbite matters more, alas, than a careful policy.

49
00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:22.314
What should voters do?

50
00:03:23.617 --> 00:03:27.416
It is essential that every voter question what lies beneath the slogan.

51
00:03:28.717 --> 00:03:31.459
Even if it be the candidate they support?

52
00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:37.724
Especially then. Come what may, critical listening is a citizen's first duty.

53
00:03:39.017 --> 00:03:43.431
Grammar focus: Subjunctive and archaic forms (extended).

54
00:03:43.731 --> 00:03:46.694
The subjunctive gives formal and rhetorical weight.

55
00:03:46.994 --> 00:03:52.198
It is essential that every voter question the slogan, even if it be their own candidate's.

56
00:03:52.498 --> 00:03:56.239
Come what may, critical listening is a citizen's first duty.

57
00:03:56.539 --> 00:04:04.611
The mandative subjunctive (that + base verb) is standard in formal English; 'if it be' is archaic and rhetorical.

58
00:04:05.617 --> 00:04:09.326
Now read and listen. The Art of Political Persuasion.

59
00:04:09.626 --> 00:04:14.528
Since ancient Athens, political rhetoric has been central to democratic life.

60
00:04:15.817 --> 00:04:21.752
Modern campaigns, however, rely on professional campaign messaging tested on focus groups.

61
00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:29.368
Spin doctors present every event in the most favourable light, while ministers repeat agreed talking points.

62
00:04:30.683 --> 00:04:36.932
In the media age, a memorable soundbite can matter more than a detailed policy.

63
00:04:38.217 --> 00:04:43.966
Some politicians use dog-whistle politics, sending coded messages to particular groups.

64
00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:50.756
Populist rhetoric, meanwhile, divides society into 'the people' and 'the elite'.

65
00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:58.183
Every electoral strategy involves persuasion, and persuasion is not in itself dishonest.

66
00:04:59.483 --> 00:05:05.500
It is vital, nevertheless, that citizens learn to distinguish argument from manipulation.

67
00:05:06.783 --> 00:05:09.552
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:09.852 --> 00:05:14.069
An adviser who presents news favourably for a politician is a blank.

69
00:05:14.369 --> 00:05:20.542
A, spin doctor. B, talking point. C, soundbite.

70
00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:27.362
The answer is A: spin doctor.

71
00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:32.045
It is essential that every citizen blank the facts.

72
00:05:32.345 --> 00:05:35.616
A, check. B, checks. C, checked.

73
00:05:39.617 --> 00:05:41.361
The answer is A: check.

74
00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:44.733
'Come what may' means blank.

75
00:05:45.033 --> 00:05:49.395
A, whatever happens. B, perhaps. C, until now.

76
00:05:53.383 --> 00:05:55.987
The answer is A: whatever happens.

77
00:05:57.483 --> 00:05:59.970
Coded messages for certain groups are blank.

78
00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:06.548
A, dog-whistle politics. B, electoral strategy. C, campaign messaging.

79
00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:13.711
The answer is A: dog-whistle politics.

80
00:06:15.217 --> 00:06:19.979
According to the text, what must citizens learn to distinguish?

81
00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:26.371
A, argument from manipulation. B, left from right. C, fact from fiction novels.

82
00:06:30.383 --> 00:06:33.811
The answer is A: argument from manipulation.

83
00:06:35.317 --> 00:06:41.348
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can young voters protect themselves from political manipulation?

84
00:06:46.648 --> 00:06:52.258
Part two: Use the mandative subjunctive and one fixed subjunctive expression.

85
00:06:58.558 --> 00:07:09.003
It is essential that young voters check facts and compare sources. I would recommend that every student follow more than one news outlet. Come what may, we must think for ourselves.

86
00:07:09.303 --> 00:07:12.022
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:13.017 --> 00:07:18.850
Great work today! You learned eight new words and subjunctive and archaic forms (extended).

88
00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:23.045
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:23.345 --> 00:07:27.797
See you in Unit thirty-seven. Goodbye!

90
00:07:28.097 --> 00:07:31.167
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

